{"description":"Jane Fonda playfully critiques Barbra Streisand's Oscars tribute to Robert Redford, highlighting her own collaborations with the late actor.","articleSummary":"During the 98th Academy Awards, Jane Fonda joked to Entertainment Tonight that she had worked with Robert Redford in four films while Streisand had only worked with him once, and that she had “more to say.”  Streisand delivered a moving tribute during the In Memoriam segment, performing a section of “The Way We Were” and praising Redford’s advocacy for press freedom, the environment, and independent cinema.

Redford, who died in September 2025 at age 89, had long collaborated with Fonda in films such as *The Chase* (1966), *Barefoot in the Park* (1967), *The Electric Horseman* (1979), and *Our Souls at Night* (2017).  Both actresses posted heartfelt tributes on Instagram, with Fonda recalling their early partnership and calling Redford a “real difference” who revolutionized independent filmmaking.

The Oscars were hosted by Conan O’Brien at Dolby Theatre, and the article notes that Streisand’s tribute included remarks about Redford’s intellect and courage, as well as his impact on the film industry.","points":["Jane Fonda said she worked with Robert Redford in four films, while Barbra Streisand worked with him in only one film.","Barbra Streisand performed a section of “The Way We Were” and praised Redford’s defense of press freedom, protection of the environment, and support of independent filmmaking during the Oscars In Memoriam tribute.","Robert Redford died in September 2025 at age 89.","Fonda and Redford co‑starred in *The Chase* (1966), *Barefoot in the Park* (1967), *The Electric Horseman* (1979), and *Our Souls at Night* (2017).","After Redford’s death, both Fonda and Streisand posted tributes on Instagram, with Fonda reflecting on their collaborations and calling him a “real difference.”"],"biasSummary":"The article adopts a celebratory tone toward Robert Redford, featuring only positive remarks from Fonda and Streisand and highlighting his achievements.  It offers no critical perspective or alternative viewpoints, indicating a bias toward a favorable remembrance of the actor."}
